WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.340
What happened to postmodernism?

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.340
That great intellectual movement of the late 20th Century

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.160
that questioned all objective conceptions of truth?

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.320
What happened to it?
Did it end?

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.680
Most people seem to agree that-
Yeah, it did end...

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:21.920
But if you ask those same people, when postmodernism ended...

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.000
they can't really give you an answer.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
This may be due to the fact that the idea of postmodernism is still

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:29.440
pretty foggy.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.520
It's hard to define it, except as a reaction to the modernism it was created to refute.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.360
The simplest way I can describe it is like this:

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.800
Postmodernism is a good "catch-all"

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.920
for all the various and chaotic ways of thinking that characterize the later part of the 20th century.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.860
But as we neared the millennium,

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.680
and the need to react to or refute modernism seemed

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.160
less and less relevant.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.680
Postmodernism sort of fell apart and we were left with...

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:20.700
well...

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:21.600
... Nothing.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.520
Indeed, one of the key characteristics of our time

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:29.120
is its inability to define itself with a set of intelletual ideas.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.840
Why is this? Why have categories like this broken down?

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.760
Why don't they seem to suit the contemporary age?

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.520
Is it because everything's just too chaotic now?

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.360
Or is it because we have too much information, and too much access for any one movement

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.520
to congeal and rise to prominence?

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.800
Yeah. I think that's probably true,

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.960
but it only tells us so much.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.280
If we dig a little bit deeper, we can see that what's really changed

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.360
is our relationship to history.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.480
As science fiction author, Bruce Sterling, once said

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.080
What Sterling means is that 'History'

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.680
studying it, or writing it, or knowing it,

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.400
has always been a game of meanings.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.880
We look back, and based on certain material facts and contemporary writings,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.800
try to tell a coherent story about past cultures and their values.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.920
The meanings they ascribe to their time and existence.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.240
Now, modernism and postmodernism had something in common

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.080
their access to history was enabled

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.400
by paper. By books. Books with covers and bindings

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.200
but more importantly with beginnings, middles, and ends.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:43.160
Modernist &amp; postmodernist thinkers had to resort to books. They had no other choice.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.240
They learned the authoritative sources on a given subject,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.640
either to accept or refute them,

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.840
and by responding to the tradition,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.440
They, themselves, became a part of it.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.480
But these are different times.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.520
The Book was replaced...

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.960
it was replaced with The Network.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.160
And the Network doesn't respect history or tradition.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.360
Let's say you wanna know something about... I don't know...

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.400
The Philosophy of Stoicism.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.880
I bet the very first thing you would do is open up a search engine, probably Google,

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:19.040
and let a complex, ever-changing algorithm, based on views, recency, filters, phrasing, and much more

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.880
prioritize a list of results.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.640
Within that list are all kinds of different and contradictory positions

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.720
all the way from top university professors to some guy on his couch.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.600
And in each article there is usually a comments section

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.760
with opinions about those opinions.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.680
Then if you can find the author's name, you can go back to Google and type that in

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.000
see if you can somehow determine via their wikipedia page or number of twitter followers

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.000
what other people consider the level of their authenticity to be.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.200
Or you can just go to YouTube, which I often do,

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.880
and see if someone's made a video about Stoicism that's a little bit easier to digest.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.320
In the network culture, everything is fluid.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.960
Poorly organized but extremely persuasive  narratives accumulate,

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.480
alter and change as quickly as Google's algorithm does.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.480
And we have access to all of them.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.040
Instantaneously.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:17.040
Sterling and fellow author, William Gibson, named this unnameable transition period of culture

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.420
Atemporality.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.360
in which we have this great unmooring from historical knowledge.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.960
This makes for a lot of unease in our time.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.840
The maps we have: The Network,

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.600
The Internet and all its social function

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:37.960
Can't seem to do its job. It can't map a strong master narrative onto the world we see in front of us.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.360
Now, maybe someday it will. Sterling gives it ten years.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.320
But it won't look like any of the master narratives from literary culture.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:51.920
Really, we have no idea what a master narrative in network culture could look like.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:57.320
Now, it's easy to see that such a disruptive break from the past also means

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.840
an uncertainty about the future.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.720
About what progress looks like...

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.760
A lack of confidence in the market, in nation states, in groups of nation states like the EU

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:13.080
in the belief that democracy can be exported around the globe are all symptomatic of this

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.080
Atemporal transition period.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:22.040
In the world and in the Arts, what you get is this collaging of past and future in the present.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:27.760
The re-purposing of old structures and obsessions with predicted futures of the past that

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:28.960
never came about.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.640
I mean what's more out-of-time, for example, than stuff like steampunk.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:38.240
It's tempting to see this new historical situation as a problem.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:41.600
But you can look at it as an opportunity too.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.600
Why not take up the whirling elements of the past and the future

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.120
and design the contempoary moment that you want to see?

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.520
In art, in your personal life, or in the public space.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.160
Maybe the high-brow philosophy of the future can't be canonized.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.940
Maybe there never will be another Charles Dickens or George Orwell,

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.760
but there's a great freedom in losing the canon, as well.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:10.120
As Sterling notes, historians really used to worry that their interpretations of the past

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:13.360
were clouded by their own values.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:16.560
Maybe the good is that for us, right now,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.280
we just don't know what our values really are.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.160
So that whether we're looking back or looking forward, the Atemporal age and

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.120
the Atemporal person can finally see

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.800
with clear eyes.

